WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.426
Operationally, we care about a process that breaks problems into steps.

2
00:00:04.426 --> 00:00:06.216
It consumes resources and time.

3
00:00:06.216 --> 00:00:08.652
And it can fail systematically.

4
00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:22.394
RLVR uses tasks with verifiable outcomes to turn correctness into a reward signal.

5
00:00:22.394 --> 00:00:27.087
DeepSeek R1 uses GRPO to compare attempts at the same problem relative to one another.

6
00:00:27.087 --> 00:00:32.103
At inference time, the model can spend more steps before producing the final answer.

7
00:00:32.103 --> 00:00:34.861
Training and inference are two different stages.

8
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.458
An answer can use a single sample

9
00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:45.216
or combine several and decide by consensus.

10
00:00:45.216 --> 00:00:48.297
Those are different strategies and should be measured separately.

11
00:00:48.297 --> 00:00:51.700
On AIME 2024, o1 scored 74% with one sample

12
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.458
and 83% with consensus across 64 samples.

13
00:00:54.458 --> 00:00:57.861
That is evidence from that experiment, not a general guarantee.

14
00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.684
Models can produce deliberate chains and still collapse when complexity or the available budget changes.

15
00:01:08.684 --> 00:01:13.055
Results also depend on benchmark and evaluation design.

16
00:01:13.055 --> 00:01:18.071
The useful practice is to measure where reasoning works, what it costs and how it fails.
